Java Testing Rules and Best Practices

Description

This document defines a practical set of rules and industry-aligned best practices for writing high-quality automated tests in Java. Its purpose is to provide developers with a consistent reference for designing tests that are reliable, readable, maintainable, deterministic, and capable of providing fast and actionable feedback.

The rules cover the main areas of automated testing, including unit testing, test structure, naming, assertions, test isolation, parameterized testing, boundary and error testing, dependency management, mocking, test speed, flaky tests, regression testing, code coverage, and the separation of unit, integration, and end-to-end tests.

These guidelines are not intended to impose arbitrary metrics, such as a mandatory minimum number of test methods per file. Instead, they prioritize meaningful coverage of application behavior, business rules, edge cases, failure scenarios, and areas with significant technical or business risk.

The recommendations in this document are based primarily on established Java testing practices and guidance from JUnit, Google’s software testing guidance, Oracle Java resources, and general industry principles such as the testing pyramid and behavior-focused testing.

