Scope

These rules should be used as development guidelines rather than absolute requirements. Teams may adapt them according to the architecture, criticality, regulatory requirements, performance constraints, and risk profile of each project.

When a specific project, organization, framework, or regulatory standard defines stricter testing requirements, those requirements should take precedence over the recommendations in this document.


List of rules

Rule 1: Write tests for behavior, not implementation details.
Rule 2: Each test should verify one clear behavior or scenario.
Rule 3: Use descriptive test names that explain the expected behavior.
Rule 4: Follow the Arrange–Act–Assert (AAA) structure whenever possible.
Rule 5: Tests must be deterministic: the same input should always produce the same result.
Rule 6: Tests must be independent and must not rely on execution order.
Rule 7: Keep unit tests fast enough to run frequently during development.
Rule 8: Avoid unnecessary shared mutable state between tests.
Rule 9: Test normal cases, boundary cases, invalid inputs, and error conditions.
Rule 10: Prefer meaningful assertions over merely checking that code executed without throwing an exception.
Rule 11: Do not enforce an arbitrary minimum number of test functions per file; write as many tests as needed to cover meaningful behaviors and risks.
Rule 12: Prefer multiple small, focused tests over one large test covering many unrelated scenarios.
Rule 13: Test public behavior and contracts whenever possible; avoid coupling tests to private implementation details.
Rule 14: A test should be understandable without reading the production implementation.
Rule 15: Use expressive names following a pattern such as method_condition_expectedResult.
Rule 16: Make test failures actionable: the test name and assertion failure should clearly indicate what is broken.
Rule 17: Prefer narrow assertions that verify the specific behavior under test.
Rule 18: Avoid asserting every field of a complex object unless the complete object state is the actual contract being tested.
Rule 19: Use assertThrows to verify expected exceptions and validate relevant exception details when they are part of the contract.
Rule 20: Do not use exceptions as the only proof that a test passed; verify the resulting state or behavior as well when appropriate.
Rule 21: Use parameterized tests when the same behavior must be verified with multiple input values.
Rule 22: Include representative normal cases, edge cases, boundary values, empty values, null values where supported, and invalid inputs.
Rule 23: Use equivalence classes and boundary-value analysis to select test data instead of creating many redundant tests.
Rule 24: Keep test data minimal and focused on the scenario being tested.
Rule 25: Avoid magic numbers and unexplained literals in tests; use named constants when they improve readability.
Rule 26: Prefer realistic domain values over meaningless values when realism improves understanding.
Rule 27: Do not share mutable state between tests unless it is strictly controlled and reset.
Rule 28: Each test must be able to run in isolation. JUnit's default per-method lifecycle supports this principle.
Rule 29: Never make a test depend on another test having run before it.
Rule 30: Do not rely on test execution order unless testing order itself is the explicit purpose.
Rule 31: Clean up files, database records, temporary resources, and other side effects created by tests.
Rule 32: Avoid using production databases, production services, or other uncontrolled external resources in unit tests.
Rule 33: Keep unit tests hermetic whenever possible: control all relevant dependencies and inputs.
Rule 34: Mock or fake external dependencies only when isolation is necessary.
Rule 35: Do not mock the class or behavior that is actually being tested.
Rule 36: Avoid excessive mocking; tests with large numbers of mocks are often a sign of excessive coupling.
Rule 37: Prefer testing observable outputs and state over verifying every internal interaction.
Rule 38: Verify interactions with mocks only when the interaction itself is part of the required behavior.
Rule 39: Do not verify unnecessary method-call counts or call order when the contract does not require them.
Rule 40: Use stubs, fakes, or real lightweight implementations when they make tests clearer than mocks.
Rule 41: Keep test setup smaller than the behavior being tested whenever possible.
Rule 42: Extract repeated test setup into helper methods, builders, or fixtures only when doing so improves readability.
Rule 43: Do not hide important scenario details behind overly generic helper methods.
Rule 44: Use @BeforeEach for common setup only when every test genuinely needs it.
Rule 45: Keep the setup, action, and assertions close enough that the test's cause and effect are obvious.
Rule 46: One test should normally validate one logical behavior, even if it contains several closely related assertions.
Rule 47: Avoid multiple unrelated scenarios in the same test method.
Rule 48: Do not use random data unless the random seed is fixed, captured, or the test is otherwise reproducible.
Rule 49: Do not depend on the current date, time zone, locale, environment variables, or machine configuration unless they are explicitly controlled.
Rule 50: Inject or abstract clocks when testing time-dependent logic.
Rule 51: Avoid Thread.sleep() in tests; use synchronization mechanisms, controlled clocks, or polling with explicit timeouts instead.
Rule 52: Treat flaky tests as defects and fix or remove the source of nondeterminism.
Rule 53: A test that passes only sometimes is not a reliable safety net.
Rule 54: Keep unit tests fast enough to run continuously during local development and CI.
Rule 55: Separate unit, integration, and end-to-end tests according to their scope and execution requirements.
Rule 56: Build a strong base of unit tests, then use integration tests to verify collaboration between components.
Rule 57: Use end-to-end tests primarily for critical user journeys rather than duplicating every unit-test scenario.
Rule 58: Test error paths as deliberately as successful paths.
Rule 59: For every important validation rule, include at least one test proving that invalid input is rejected correctly.
Rule 60: For every important business rule, include tests for both the condition being satisfied and the condition not being satisfied.
Rule 61: Test boundary values explicitly, especially minimum, maximum, just-below, and just-above relevant limits.
Rule 62: Add regression tests for bugs that have been fixed whenever the bug can be reproduced automatically.
Rule 63: A regression test should fail before the fix and pass after the fix.
Rule 64: Do not delete a failing test simply to make the build green; first determine whether the test or the production behavior is incorrect.
Rule 65: Keep tests under version control together with the production code they validate.
Rule 66: New production behavior should normally be accompanied by corresponding automated tests.
Rule 67: Review test code with the same level of care as production code.
Rule 68: Refactor tests when duplication or complexity makes their intent difficult to understand.
Rule 69: Do not over-abstract tests; duplication is sometimes clearer than a complicated test framework.
Rule 70: Prefer readable test code over clever or overly compact test code.
Rule 71: Keep assertion messages useful when the assertion framework does not already provide sufficient diagnostic information.
Rule 72: Make failures easy to diagnose without requiring unnecessary reruns or additional logging.
Rule 73: Use test names that describe the expected outcome rather than generic names such as test1, testMethod, or shouldWork.
Rule 74: Avoid testing framework code, trivial getters/setters, or generated code unless they contain meaningful custom behavior or risk.
Rule 75: Prioritize tests based on business impact, complexity, likelihood of failure, and consequences of defects.
Rule 76: Code coverage is a useful signal, not a quality metric by itself.
Rule 77: Do not write meaningless tests solely to increase line or branch coverage.
Rule 78: A high coverage percentage does not guarantee that important behaviors or edge cases are tested.
Rule 79: Use mutation testing or equivalent techniques when stronger evidence of test effectiveness is required.
Rule 80: Keep tests maintainable so that legitimate production refactoring does not require widespread test rewrites.
Rule 81: When implementation changes but externally observable behavior does not, behavior-focused tests should generally remain unchanged.
Rule 82: Use @ParameterizedTest instead of duplicating nearly identical test methods for the same behavior with different data.
Rule 83: Use descriptive parameter names and display names so that individual parameterized test failures can be identified easily.
Rule 84: Group related scenarios using nested test classes only when the hierarchy improves readability.
Rule 85: Do not create test classes so large that finding a specific behavior becomes difficult.
Rule 86: Organize tests by the behavior or component they validate rather than by arbitrary alphabetical or historical order.
Rule 87: Keep production code testable by favoring clear responsibilities and explicit dependencies.
Rule 88: If a class is extremely difficult to test, investigate whether its design has too many responsibilities or hidden dependencies.
Rule 89: Prefer dependency injection over directly creating hard-to-control external dependencies inside business logic.
Rule 90: Avoid static global state when it makes tests difficult to isolate.
Rule 91: Test concurrency-sensitive code with deterministic synchronization and clearly defined expectations.
Rule 92: Do not rely solely on repeated execution to prove concurrent code is correct.
Rule 93: Set explicit timeouts for tests that may block, but do not use timeouts as a substitute for correct synchronization.
Rule 94: Ensure tests can run consistently on developer machines and in CI environments.
Rule 95: Avoid tests that require undocumented local setup or manually installed dependencies.
Rule 96: Use test tags or categories to separate fast tests from slower integration or system tests when the build supports it.
Rule 97: Run fast unit tests on every relevant code change whenever possible.
Rule 98: Run broader integration and end-to-end suites at appropriate stages of the delivery pipeline.
Rule 99: Treat slow, flaky, opaque, or difficult-to-maintain tests as technical debt.
Rule 100: The ultimate standard for a good test is that it provides fast, reliable, understandable, and actionable feedback about meaningful software behavior.